+# Alamgir et al. 2022 - Heavy metals in unbranded whitening creams sold in Karachi
+
+This study measured Pb, Ni, Cr, As, and Cu in 25 unbranded whitening creams from Karachi local markets. Pb was the dominant reported metal, with sample values from 2.11 to 9.12 ppm and a mean of 4.568 ppm. The source is routeable to skin-lightening and leave-on cosmetic product pages as finished-product occurrence evidence.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+Table I reports concentrations in ppm:
+
+| Metal | Mean | SD | Min-max |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| Pb | 4.568 | 1.847 | 2.11-9.12 |
+| Ni | 0.546 | 0.274 | 0.12-1.21 |
+| Cr | 2.013 | 0.421 | 1.13-2.68 |
+| tAs | 0.022 | 0.021 | 0.005-0.091 |
+| Cu | 0.489 | 0.145 | 0.22-0.85 |
+
+The paper reports the relative mean order as Pb > Cr > Ni > Cu > As across the 25 samples.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+Twenty-five unbranded cream samples were collected from three Karachi markets, oven dried, digested with nitric acid, and analyzed with Merck Super Nova60 kits according to the paper's method section. Results were measured in triplicate and reported as mean values and standard deviations.
+
+## Implications
+
+Unbranded skin-whitening creams in this Karachi sample set had measurable Pb in every sample and measurable Cr, Ni, As, and Cu. The source should remain jurisdiction-specific and product-class-specific; it is not evidence for branded or regulated-market products outside the sampled setting.
+
+## Wiki pages this source may touch
+
