+# Hosojima et al. 2017 - Cadmium in rice endosperm protein powder used in a supplementation trial
+
+This Scientific Reports clinical trial is not primarily a contaminants survey, but Table 1 provides routeable finished-ingredient composition for the rice endosperm protein (REP) powder used in the trial. The occurrence-relevant value is Cd 0.14 +/- 0.17 ppm across five REP powder batches, measured after processing intended to remove potassium and cadmium.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+Table 1 reports the composition of REP powder as average of five batches +/- SD:
+
+| Parameter | REP powder |
+|---|---:|
+| Water | 2.5 +/- 0.3% |
+| Protein | 91.2 +/- 1.0% |
+| Phosphorus | 95 +/- 16 mg/100 g |
+| Potassium | <1 mg/100 g |
+| Sodium | 17.3 +/- 2.7 mg/100 g |
+| Calcium | 31.4 +/- 5.3 mg/100 g |
+| Magnesium | 5.1 +/- 0.7 mg/100 g |
+| Cadmium | 0.14 +/- 0.17 ppm |
+
+Soy and casein protein reference values in the same table do not report cadmium and should not be used as Cd occurrence evidence.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+REP was extracted from Japonica rice flour, washed four times with diluted HCl after pH adjustment to remove minerals and heavy metals such as potassium and cadmium, neutralized, pasteurized, dehydrated, and lyophilized. Cadmium, potassium, and sodium were measured by atomic absorption spectrophotometry; calcium and magnesium by ICP-AES.
+
+## Implications
+
+The source supports Cd occurrence context for rice-derived protein supplement ingredients, especially processed rice protein isolates. It does not provide a retail market distribution, and the clinical endpoints should stay outside product-occurrence routing.
+
